    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> I Like School!</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like Math!</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Engage in interactive labeling activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple words related to school preferences with model guidance (e.g., writing 'games' or 'math' by copying from a model or example). Can copy and trace letters related to school activities (e.g., tracing 'P' for 'painting,' 'A' for 'Art'). Can draw and label favorite school activities collaboratively (e.g., drawing a math book and labeling it 'math').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Good morning, everyone! Let's talk about school!" The teacher shows flashcards of different school activities (e.g., math, art, playground, music). The teacher asks, "Do you like math?" and models saying "Yes, I like math!" or "No, I don't like math." Students respond by pointing and saying simple phrases. Teacher then points to a flashcard of 'games' and emphasizes the /g/ sound. Then, points to 'like' and emphasizes the /l/ sound. The teacher then shows a simple picture of a child writing and asks, "What is she doing? She is writing!" (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a large picture of a math book. "Look! A math book! I like math!" The teacher takes a marker and, in front of the class, slowly traces the word "math" written in large, dashed letters on a piece of paper, saying each letter sound as she traces (e.g., "/m/ /a/ /t/ /h/"). Then, the teacher demonstrates copying the word "math" next to the picture, emphasizing correct letter formation and spacing. (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I drawing or writing now?" (Expected: "Writing!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this 'math' or 'art'?" (Pointing to the word "math") (Expected: "Math!")</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher ensures students understand the concept of tracing and copying words related to school activities. (2 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I Like School!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large classroom poster or digital presentation showing simple, clear illustrations of various school activities: a math book with numbers, a paint palette, children playing in a playground, musical notes, science experiment items, and a teacher reading an English book. Each illustration has an empty label box underneath it.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner can understand short, very simple messages and questions related to daily routines and preferences, and identify visual information (e.g., school subjects).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to each illustration and asks, "What's this? Is this math?" Students chorally respond "Yes, math!" or "No, that's art!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "Who likes music?" and students raise their hands. "I like music!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows pre-written word cards (math, art, games, music) and asks students to match them orally to the correct picture on the poster.</li>
                </ul> (8 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing simple words for school subjects and activities (e.g., 'math', 'art', 'games'). They will reinforce the use of 'I like...' orally while writing. Grammar: Subject + verb ('I like...'). Vocabulary: math, art, games, enjoy, count, write.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Tracing Fun:</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with large, dashed-line versions of the words "math," "art," "games," and "music." Each word is accompanied by a simple drawing. Students use crayons or pencils to trace the letters of each word. The teacher walks around, providing individual support and checking letter formation, reminding students of the /l/ sound in 'like' and /g/ sound in 'games'. (7 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Copy and Match:</b> Students receive a new worksheet with pictures of a math book, an easel for art, children playing games, and a musical note. Below each picture, there is a blank line. At the top of the worksheet, there is a word bank with "math," "art," "games," and "music" clearly written. Students copy the correct word from the word bank onto the line next to each corresponding picture. The teacher encourages students to say "I like math" as they copy the word. (7 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Labeling:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of picture cards (math book, art supplies, playground, music instruments) and blank small paper strips. One student picks a card, shows it to their partner, and says, "I like [art]!" Then, both students try to write the word 'art' on a paper strip, using the word bank from the previous activity or models on the board as a guide. They then place the written label next to the picture. (6 min)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I Like School!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Drawing and Labeling. Students will create a drawing of their favorite school activity and label it with the corresponding word. This connects directly to the Theme: "I Like Math!" and Scenario: "I Like School!".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. My Favorite Activity:</b> The teacher gives each student a blank sheet of paper and asks, "What is your favorite school activity? Do you like math? Do you like art? Do you like games?" The teacher displays flashcards of 'math', 'art', 'games', 'music', 'playground' on the board with their written words as models.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Draw and Color:</b> Students draw their favorite school activity on their paper and color it. For example, a student might draw a math book with numbers, a child on a swing, or a person painting.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Label It!:</b> The teacher then instructs, "Now, let's write what you like!" Students attempt to label their drawing by copying the word from the model words displayed on the board (e.g., writing 'math' next to their math book drawing). The teacher circulates, offering support, encouragement, and helping to spell out words if needed, ensuring focus on phonemic awareness for words like 'math' and 'games'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>4. Share and Express:</b> Students hold up their labeled drawings and, if they wish, briefly say "I like [math]!" or "I like [art]!" to a partner or the class.</li>
                </ul> (10 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the production phase and review of student's labeled drawings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My Favorite School Activity" drawing with the written label.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can copy and trace letters related to school activities (e.g., 'math', 'art', 'games').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can write simple words related to school preferences with model guidance.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows an attempt to label their drawing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Optional: Intelligible pronunciation when sharing "I like..." orally).</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I trace all the words?" (Thumbs up/down)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I try to write my favorite school activity?" (Thumbs up/down)</li>
                </ul> (5 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word did you like to write the most today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you write 'math' or 'art' by yourself?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this writing lesson for Kinder? The use of tracing and copying from models was very effective. Students were engaged in drawing their favorite activities. Many students successfully traced and copied simple words like 'math' and 'art'. Some struggled with letter formation for 'games' and needed more individual guidance. Adjustments needed for the next lesson could include providing laminated tracing cards for repeated practice or focusing on one or two new letters at a time. More visual aids for less common words (e.g., 'science') would be beneficial. The focus on /l/ and /g/ sounds during tracing helped connect pronunciation to writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will build on these labeled drawings. Students can use their labeled drawings to present their preferences, potentially guiding a partner through their drawing and explaining why they like that activity, consolidating their learning in a project-based activity. They will mediate information from their personal drawing into a simple oral presentation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>